IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cda-nouns`

-- Criminal Damage Act 1971, sections 6, 7 and 9: SEARCH, TRIAL AND EVIDENCE.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Written from the text alone.
--
-- These sections address a justice of the peace, a constable and the courts, not the
-- person suspected or charged. Each is encoded for what it decides, and the judgments
-- the text hands to an official are conditional inputs, never guessed:
--   * s 6(1): whether it "is made to appear" to the justice that there is "reasonable
--     cause to believe" something is a judgment of the justice, so it is asked as
--     "if it is made to appear ...";
--   * s 6(2): the constable's powers follow from being authorised, and from what the
--     constable "believes";
--   * s 7(2) and s 9: the answer is a value of a small named type, with an explicit
--     "says nothing about this" value for cases the section does not reach.
-- Section 7(1) and section 8 are blank in this text (dots only), and are not encoded.

§ `Section 6 -- Search for things intended for use in committing offences of criminal damage`

§§ `6(1) -- the justice's power to grant a warrant`

-- src:35 | 6 Search for things intended for use in committing offences of criminal damage.
-- src:36 | (1) If it is made to appear by information on oath before a justice of the peace that there is reasonable cause to believe that any person has in his custody or under his control or on his premises anything which there is reasonable cause to believe has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse—
-- src:37 | (a) to destroy or damage property belonging to another; or
-- src:38 | (b) to destroy or damage any property in a way likely to endanger the life of another,
-- src:39 | the justice may grant a warrant authorising any constable to search for and seize that thing.
@ref s 6(1) (src:35-39)
GIVEN `it is made to appear by information on oath before a justice of the peace` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is reasonable cause to believe that the person has the thing in his custody or under his control or on his premises` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is reasonable cause to believe that the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage property belonging to another` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is reasonable cause to believe that the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage any property in a way likely to endanger the life of another` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a justice of the peace may grant a warrant authorising any constable to search for and seize the thing` IF
        `it is made to appear by information on oath before a justice of the peace`
    AND `there is reasonable cause to believe that the person has the thing in his custody or under his control or on his premises`
    AND (   `there is reasonable cause to believe that the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage property belonging to another`
         OR `there is reasonable cause to believe that the thing has been used or is intended for use without lawful excuse to destroy or damage any property in a way likely to endanger the life of another`)

§§ `6(2) -- what an authorised constable may do`

-- src:40 | (2) A constable who is authorised under this section to search premises for anything, may enter (if need be by force) and search the premises accordingly and may seize anything which he believes to have been used or to be intended to be used as aforesaid.
@ref s 6(2) (src:40)
GIVEN `the constable is authorised under section 6 to search the premises for the thing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the constable may enter the premises, by force if need be, and search them` IF
    `the constable is authorised under section 6 to search the premises for the thing`

-- "may seize anything which he believes to have been used or to be intended to be used
-- as aforesaid": the power is not confined to the thing the warrant names, and it
-- turns on the constable's belief, not on whether the belief is right.
@ref s 6(2) (src:40)
GIVEN `the constable is authorised under section 6 to search the premises for something` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the constable believes the thing has been used or is intended to be used as described in section 6(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the constable may seize the thing` IF
        `the constable is authorised under section 6 to search the premises for something`
    AND `the constable believes the thing has been used or is intended to be used as described in section 6(1)`

§§ `6(3) -- what happens to what the police take`

DECLARE `Law governing property that came into police possession under section 6` IS ONE OF
    `the Police (Property) Act 1897 applies, as it applies to property that has come into the possession of the police in the circumstances mentioned in that Act`
    `section 6(3) says nothing about this`

-- src:41 | (3) The Police (Property) Act 1897 (disposal of property in the possession of the police) shall apply to property which has come into the possession of the police under this section as it applies to property which has come into the possession of the police in the circumstances mentioned in that Act.
@ref s 6(3) (src:41)
GIVEN `the property has come into the possession of the police under section 6` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Law governing property that came into police possession under section 6`
DECIDE `the law governing property that has come into the possession of the police under section 6` IS
    IF `the property has come into the possession of the police under section 6`
    THEN `the Police (Property) Act 1897 applies, as it applies to property that has come into the possession of the police in the circumstances mentioned in that Act`
    ELSE `section 6(3) says nothing about this`

§ `Section 7 -- Jurisdiction of magistrates' courts`

DECLARE `Effect of a dispute of title on the jurisdiction of magistrates' courts` IS ONE OF
    `that rule of law does not preclude a magistrates' court from trying the offence`
    `section 7(2) says nothing about this`

-- src:42 | 7 Jurisdiction of magistrates’ courts.
-- src:44 | (2) No rule of law ousting the jurisdiction of magistrates’ courts to try offences where a dispute of title to property is involved shall preclude magistrates’ courts from trying offences under this Act, or any other offences of destroying or damaging property.
@ref s 7(2) (src:42-44)
GIVEN `the rule is a rule of law ousting the jurisdiction of magistrates' courts to try offences where a dispute of title to property is involved` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence is an offence under this Act or any other offence of destroying or damaging property` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Effect of a dispute of title on the jurisdiction of magistrates' courts`
DECIDE `the effect of a rule of law about a dispute of title on the jurisdiction of magistrates' courts` IS
    IF     `the rule is a rule of law ousting the jurisdiction of magistrates' courts to try offences where a dispute of title to property is involved`
       AND `the offence is an offence under this Act or any other offence of destroying or damaging property`
    THEN `that rule of law does not preclude a magistrates' court from trying the offence`
    ELSE `section 7(2) says nothing about this`

§ `Section 9 -- Evidence in connection with offences under this Act`

DECLARE `Whether section 9 excuses a person from answering or complying` IS ONE OF
    `not excused by reason that to answer or comply may incriminate`
    `section 9 says nothing about this`

DECLARE `Against whom a statement or admission is offered` IS ONE OF
    `the person who made it`
    `the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it`

DECLARE `Admissibility under section 9` IS ONE OF
    `not admissible in evidence`
    `section 9 says nothing about this`

§§ `9, first part -- no excuse by reason of incrimination`

-- src:46 | 9 Evidence in connection with offences under this Act.
-- src:47 | A person shall not be excused, by reason that to do so may incriminate that person or the spouse or civil partner of that person of an offence under this Act—
-- src:48 | (a) from answering any question put to that person in proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property; or
-- src:49 | (b) from complying with any order made in any such proceedings;
@ref s 9(a) and (b) (src:46-49)
GIVEN `the proceedings are for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a question is put to the person in those proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an order is made in those proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
      `answering or complying may incriminate the person, or the spouse or civil partner of the person, of an offence under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Whether section 9 excuses a person from answering or complying`
DECIDE `whether the person is excused from answering or complying` IS
    IF     `the proceedings are for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property`
       AND (   `a question is put to the person in those proceedings`
            OR `an order is made in those proceedings`)
       AND `answering or complying may incriminate the person, or the spouse or civil partner of the person, of an offence under this Act`
    THEN `not excused by reason that to answer or comply may incriminate`
    ELSE `section 9 says nothing about this`

§§ `9, second part -- no use against the person or the spouse or civil partner`

-- src:50 | but no statement or admission made by a person in answering a question put or complying with an order made as aforesaid shall, in proceedings for an offence under this Act, be admissible in evidence against that person or (unless they married or became civil partners after the making of the statement or admission) against the spouse or civil partner of that person.
@ref s 9 (src:50)
GIVEN `the statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings in which it is offered are proceedings for an offence under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is offered against` IS A `Against whom a statement or admission is offered`
      `the spouse or civil partner married or became a civil partner after the statement or admission was made` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Admissibility under section 9`
DECIDE `the admissibility of the statement or admission` IS
    IF     `the statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property`
       AND `the proceedings in which it is offered are proceedings for an offence under this Act`
    THEN CONSIDER `it is offered against`
         WHEN `the person who made it` THEN `not admissible in evidence`
         WHEN `the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it` THEN
             IF `the spouse or civil partner married or became a civil partner after the statement or admission was made`
             THEN `section 9 says nothing about this`
             ELSE `not admissible in evidence`
    ELSE `section 9 says nothing about this`
